Test 1. This is the IELTS listening test.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
and you will have a chance to check your work.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
All the recordings will be played once only.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
The test is in four parts.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
At the end of the test you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Now turn to part 1.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Part 1. You will hear a teaching assistant calling a country park about a school visit.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 6.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Part 2. You will hear a teacher talking to a child in a classroom.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Part 3. You will hear a teacher talking to a child in a classroom.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Part 4. You will hear a teacher talking to a child in a classroom.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.000
...two of our classes.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.000
OK. What would you like to know?

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Well, I'm new to this area, so perhaps you could tell me something about the park first, please.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Of course. Altogether, the park covers 170 acres. That's 69 hectares.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
There are three main types of habitat – wetland, grassland and woodland.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
The woods are well established and varied, with an oak plantation and other areas of mixed species.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Right.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:42.000
The wetland is quite varied, too. The original farmland was dug up around 40 years ago to extract gravel.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Once this work was completed, the gravel pits filled with water, forming the two large lakes.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:56.000
There are also several smaller ones – ponds and a stream that flows through the park.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
OK. So I suppose with these different habitats, there's quite a variety of wildlife.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:13.000
There certainly is. A lot of different species of birds and insects, and also animals like deer and rabbits.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.000
And I understand you organise educational visits for school parties.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:26.000
That's right. We can organise a wide range of activities and adapt them to suit all ages.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Can you give me some examples of the activities?

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Well, one focus is on science, where we help children to discover and study plants, trees and insects.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.000
They also collect and analyse data about the things they see.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Aha.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Another focus is on geography. The park is a great environment to learn and practise reading a map

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
and using a compass to navigate around the park.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Do you do anything connected with history?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Yes, we do. For instance, the children can explore how the use of the land has changed over time.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Then there's leisure and tourism.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
That focuses on your visitors, I would imagine.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Yes, mostly. The children find out about them, their requirements, the problems they may cause and how we manage these.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.000
And another subject we cover is music.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Here the children experiment with natural materials to create sounds and explore rhythm and tempo.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.000
That must be fun.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Most children really enjoy it.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 7 to 10.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Now listen and answer questions 7 to 10.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
And of course, all the activities are educational too.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Learning outside the classroom encourages children to be creative and to explore and discover for themselves.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.000
I would imagine they get a sense of freedom that might not be a normal part of their lives.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:45.000
That's right. And very often the children discover that they can do things they didn't know they could do and they develop new skills.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.000
This gives them greater self-confidence.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.000
It sounds great. So what about the practical side of it?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:02.000
How much does it cost for a full day visit? We would expect to bring between 30 and 40 children.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:11.000
If there are over 30, it costs £4.95 for each child who attends on the day.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:19.000
We invoice you afterwards so you don't pay for children who can't come because of sickness, for example.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.000
There's no charge for leaders and other adults, as many as you want to bring.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
That sounds very fair.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Well, thanks for all the information. I'll need to discuss it with my colleagues and I hope to get back to you soon to make a booking.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
We'll look forward to hearing from you. Goodbye.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Goodbye and thank you.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.000
Bye.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Bye.

